Let $F$ be an ordered field that is Archimedean ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and has the nested interval property (NIP) of [[def-completeness-properties]]. Then $F$ has the Bolzano-Weierstrass property (BW): every bounded sequence in $F$ has a subsequence converging in $F$. Say that a set $E \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is **cofinal** when for every $K \in \mathbb{N}$ there is $k \ge K$ with $k \in E$. The construction below bisects a bracketing interval, keeping at each stage a half that the sequence visits cofinally often, and reads the limit off (NIP).
