Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) with Cauchy completeness (CC). Then $F$ has the monotone convergence property (MCT) of [[def-completeness-properties]]: every nondecreasing sequence in $F$ that is bounded above converges in $F$. The Archimedean hypothesis is not decoration. Without it the implication is false: $\mathbb{R}((t^{-1}))$ has (CC) ([[thm-laurent-cauchy-complete]]) and fails (MCT), since (MCT) would force it to be Archimedean ([[lem-mct-implies-archimedean]]) and it is not ([[lem-laurent-non-archimedean]]).
