thm-cesaro-mean-theorem	theorem	"If $x_k \\to L$ then $\\sigma_n \\to L$: convergence implies $(C,1)$-summability to the same value"
Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals that converges ([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]), and let $(\sigma_n)$ be its sequence of Cesaro means ([[def-cesaro-mean]]). Then $(\sigma_n)$ converges as well, and $$\lim_n \sigma_n \;=\; \lim_k x_k .$$ Both limits are asserted to exist: the right-hand one by hypothesis, the left-hand one as part of the conclusion. Equivalently: a convergent sequence is $(C,1)$-summable, to its own limit. The notation is licensed by uniqueness of limits of real sequences ([[lem-limit-unique]]). The converse is false ([[fs-cesaro-converse]]).
