cor-cesaro-matrix-is-regular	corollary	"The Cesaro matrix satisfies the Silverman-Toeplitz conditions, giving a second proof of the Cesaro mean theorem"
Define $c_{n,k} := (n+1)^{-1}$ for $k \le n$ and $c_{n,k} := 0$ for $k > n$. Then: 1. $c$ is a summability matrix ([[def-summability-matrix]]), with $n$ an admissible bound for row $n$; 2. the transform of a sequence $(x_k)$ by $c$ is exactly its sequence of Cesaro means ([[def-cesaro-mean]]), $y_n = \sigma_n$; 3. $c$ satisfies the three conditions of [[thm-silverman-toeplitz]], so $c$ is regular. Consequently every convergent sequence has $\sigma_n \to \lim_k x_k$, which is a second proof of [[thm-cesaro-mean-theorem]], obtained from the general characterisation rather than from a direct estimate.
URLS https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silverman%E2%80%93Toeplitz_theorem | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation

