def-series	definition	"Series, partial sums, convergence and the sum, divergence, and the tail series"
Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-real-numbers]]) and a **sequence of reals** is a function $a : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-sequence]]), written $(a_k)$; recall that $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$. **Partial sums.** Let $(a_k)$ be a sequence of reals. Its **sequence of partial sums** is $$s_n \;:=\; \sum_{k < n} a_k \qquad (n \in \mathbb{N}),$$ the finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In particular $s_0 = 0$, the empty sum, and $s_{n+1} = s_n + a_n$ for every $n$, those being exactly the two recursion clauses that define the finite sum. Note that $s_n$ is the sum of the $n$ terms $a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}$, so the index 
URLS https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Series_(mathematics) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principles_of_Mathematical_Analysis | https://terrytao.wordpress.com/books/analysis-i/

lem-series-tail-invariance	lemma	"A series converges iff each of its tail series converges, and the sum splits as $s_N$ plus the $N$-th tail"
