def-uniform-space-by-entourages	definition	"Uniform space in the entourage formulation"
Let $X$ be a set and write $\Delta_X=\{(x,x):x\in X\}$ for its diagonal ([[def-the-diagonal-of-a-space]]). For $E\subseteq X\times X$, put $E[x]:=\{y\in X:(x,y)\in E\}$, $E^{-1}:=\{(y,x):(x,y)\in E\}$, and $E\circ F:=\{(x,z):\text{some }y\text{ has }(x,y)\in E,(y,z)\in F\}$. A **uniformity** on $X$ is a filter $\mathcal U$ on $X\times X$ ([[def-filter]]) such that: - every $E\in\mathcal U$ contains $\Delta_X$; - $E\in\mathcal U$ implies $E^{-1}\in\mathcal U$; - for every $E\in\mathcal U$ there is $D\in\mathcal U$ with $D\circ D\subseteq E$. Its members are **entourages**. A **uniform space** is a set equipped with a uniformity. The induced to
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-symmetric-entourages-form-a-base	lemma	"Every uniformity has a base of symmetric entourages"
If $\mathcal U$ is a uniformity on $X$, then its symmetric entourages form a filter base: for every $E\in\mathcal U$ there is a symmetric $D\in\mathcal U$ with $D\subseteq E$.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

thm-uniformity-induces-a-topology	theorem	"The sets containing an entourage ball about each of their points form a topology"
For a uniformity $\mathcal U$ on $X$, call $O\subseteq X$ open when every $x\in O$ has an entourage $E$ with $E[x]\subseteq O$. These open sets form a topology on $X$. Its neighbourhood filter at $x$ has $\{E[x]:E\in\mathcal U\}$ as a base.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-separated-uniform-space	definition	"Separated uniformity: the intersection of all entourages is the diagonal"
A uniformity $\mathcal U$ on $X$ is **separated** when $\bigcap_{E\in\mathcal U}E=\Delta_X$ ([[def-the-diagonal-of-a-space]]). Equivalently, whenever $x\ne y$, some entourage $E$ satisfies $(x,y)\notin E$. Separation is a property of the uniformity, not an additional convention in the meaning of uniform space.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

thm-separated-uniformity-iff-induced-topology-is-hausdorff	theorem	"A uniformity is separated if and only if its induced topology is Hausdorff"
The topology induced by a uniformity $\mathcal U$ is Hausdorff if and only if $\mathcal U$ is separated.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-uniformly-continuous-map	definition	"Uniformly continuous map between uniform spaces"
For uniform spaces $(X,\mathcal U_X)$ and $(Y,\mathcal U_Y)$, a map $f:X\to Y$ is **uniformly continuous** if for every $V\in\mathcal U_Y$ there is $U\in\mathcal U_X$ such that $(x,x')\in U$ implies $(f(x),f(x'))\in V$. The controlling entourage $U$ is independent of the point $x$.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

thm-uniformly-continuous-maps-are-continuous	theorem	"Every uniformly continuous map is continuous for the induced topologies"
Every uniformly continuous map between uniform spaces is continuous for their induced topologies.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-metric-uniformity-dictionary	lemma	"A metric generates an entourage uniformity whose induced topology and uniformly continuous maps are the usual metric notions, and this uniformity is separated"
For a metric space $(X,d)$, the sets $E_\varepsilon=\{(x,y):d(x,y)<\varepsilon\}$, $\varepsilon>0$, generate a separated uniformity. Its induced topology is the metric topology, and uniform continuity to another metric uniformity is exactly metric uniform continuity.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-uniform-cover-space	definition	"Uniform space in the uniform-cover formulation"
For a cover $\mathcal V$ of $X$ and $A\subseteq X$, write $\operatorname{St}(A,\mathcal V)$ for the union of the members of $\mathcal V$ meeting $A$. A cover $\mathcal V$ **star-refines** $\mathcal W$ if for every $V\in\mathcal V$, $\operatorname{St}(V,\mathcal V)$ is contained in some member of $\mathcal W$. A **uniform-cover structure** is a nonempty family $\mathfrak C$ of covers of $X$ such that a cover refined by a member of $\mathfrak C$ belongs to $\mathfrak C$, any two members have a common refinement in $\mathfrak C$, and every member has a star-refinement in $\mathfrak C$. Its members are **uniform covers**. The topology it induces 
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-entourage-and-uniform-cover-dictionary	lemma	"Entourage uniformities and uniform-cover structures determine one another"
In ZF, an entourage uniformity determines a uniform-cover structure by the covers $\{E[x]:x\in X\}$, and a uniform-cover structure determines an entourage uniformity by the sets $\bigcup_{V\in\mathcal V}V\times V$. These constructions recover the same uniform structure.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-gauge-of-pseudometrics	definition	"A gauge of pseudometrics and the uniformity it generates"
A **gauge of pseudometrics** on $X$ is a family $\mathcal P$ of pseudometrics ([[def-metric-space]]). For finite $F\subseteq\mathcal P$ and $\varepsilon>0$, put $E(F,\varepsilon)=\{(x,y):p(x,y)<\varepsilon\text{ for every }p\in F\}$. The nonempty such sets form a filter base and generate a uniformity ([[def-filter-base]], [[lem-filter-base-generates]]), called the uniformity **generated by $\mathcal P$**. For an already given uniformity $\mathcal U$ on $X$, a pseudometric $p$ is **uniformly continuous for $\mathcal U$** when $$ \{(x,y):p(x,y)<\varepsilon\}\in\mathcal U $$ for every $\varepsilon>0$. With this terminology, each member of a gaug
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages	lemma	"Assuming dependent choice, every entourage admits a normal symmetric sequence subordinate to it"
Assuming dependent choice, for every entourage $U$ there are symmetric entourages $(E_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ such that $E_0=X\times X$, $E_1\subseteq U$, the sequence is decreasing, and $E_{n+1}^{\circ3}\subseteq E_n$ for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-pseudometric-from-a-normal-entourage-sequence	lemma	"A normal sequence of entourages yields a uniformly continuous pseudometric with controlled dyadic balls"
Given a decreasing symmetric sequence $(E_n)$ with $E_0=X\times X$ and $E_{n+1}^{\circ3}\subseteq E_n$, there is a pseudometric $p$ on $X$ such that $$ E_n\subseteq\{p\le2^{-n}\}\subseteq E_{n-1} $$ for every $n\ge1$. In particular, each set $\{p<\varepsilon\}$ is an entourage, so $p$ is uniformly continuous for the original uniformity in the sense of [[def-gauge-of-pseudometrics]].
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

thm-entourage-uniformities-are-generated-by-gauges	theorem	"Assuming dependent choice, every entourage uniformity is generated by a gauge of uniformly continuous pseudometrics"
Assuming dependent choice, every entourage uniformity is generated by a gauge of uniformly continuous pseudometrics.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

thm-three-definitions-of-uniform-space-are-equivalent	theorem	"Entourages and uniform covers give equivalent definitions of a uniform space in ZF, and under dependent choice they are also equivalent to gauges of pseudometrics"
In ZF, the entourage and uniform-cover formulations determine each other. Assuming dependent choice, they are also equivalent to the formulation by gauges of pseudometrics.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-cauchy-filter-in-a-uniform-space	definition	"Cauchy filter in a uniform space"
A filter $\mathcal F$ on a uniform space $(X,\mathcal U)$ is **Cauchy** if for every entourage $E\in\mathcal U$ some $A\in\mathcal F$ satisfies $A\times A\subseteq E$. Such an $A$ is an $E$-small member of $\mathcal F$.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-convergent-filters-are-cauchy	lemma	"Every convergent filter on a uniform space is Cauchy"
Every filter converging in the induced topology of a uniform space is Cauchy.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-cauchy-filter-with-a-cluster-point-converges	lemma	"A Cauchy filter with a cluster point converges to that point"
A Cauchy filter on a uniform space with a cluster point $x$ converges to $x$.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-complete-uniform-space	definition	"Complete uniform space: every Cauchy filter converges"
A uniform space is **complete** when every Cauchy filter ([[def-cauchy-filter-in-a-uniform-space]]) converges to at least one point of its induced topology ([[def-filter-convergence-and-cluster-point]]). No separatedness is built into this definition.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-uniform-embedding-and-uniform-isomorphism	definition	"Uniform embedding and uniform isomorphism"
A map $f:X\to Y$ of uniform spaces is a **uniform embedding** if it is injective and its corestriction $X\to f[X]$, with the subspace uniformity, is a uniform isomorphism. A **uniform isomorphism** is a bijection whose map and inverse are uniformly continuous. Bijection and corestriction are understood in the sense of [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]].
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

def-completion-of-a-uniform-space	definition	"A Hausdorff completion of a uniform space and its canonical dense map"
A **Hausdorff completion** of a uniform space $(X,\mathcal U)$ is a complete separated uniform space $(\widehat X,\widehat{\mathcal U})$ together with a map $\eta:X\to\widehat X$ satisfying both of the following conditions. - The image $\eta[X]$ is dense: $\overline{\eta[X]}=\widehat X$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). - The original uniformity is exactly the uniformity pulled back along $\eta$: for every $\widehat E\in\widehat{\mathcal U}$, $(\eta\times\eta)^{-1}[\widehat E]\in\mathcal U$, and for every $E\in\mathcal U$ there is $\widehat E\in\widehat{\mathcal U}$ with $(\eta\times\eta)^{-1}[\widehat E]\subseteq E$. The first half of
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-associated-to-a-cauchy-filter	lemma	"Every Cauchy filter canonically determines a unique minimal Cauchy filter coarser than it"
Every Cauchy filter $\mathcal F$ canonically determines a unique Cauchy filter $m(\mathcal F)\subseteq\mathcal F$ that has no strictly coarser Cauchy filter.
URLS https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf

lem-entourages-on-minimal-cauchy-filters-form-a-separated-uniformity	lemma	"The standard entourages on minimal Cauchy filters form a separated uniformity"
On the set $\widehat X$ of minimal Cauchy filters, the relations $\widehat E$ declaring that two filters have $E$-close members form a separated uniformity.
